Title: the CCL test lanes exit 0 even when tests fail
Status: open
Found: 2026-09-16, while filing what was reported as a "flaky" alfe CCL test
Related: issues/open/alfe-bricscad-template-path-escaped-on-ccl.issue
         (the failure this has been hiding)
Affects: clautolisp/Makefile test-ccl, autolisp-front-end/Makefile test-ccl

SYMPTOM

The alfe CCL lane printed

    Did 679 checks.
       Pass: 678 (99%)
       Fail: 1 ( 0%)

and exited 0. So did the same lane on clean master. A red test, and a
green lane.

CAUSE -- measured, not inferred

Both CCL lanes are invoked as

    ccl --no-init --quiet \
        --eval '...load...' \
        --eval '(asdf:test-system "..." :verbose t)' \
        --eval '(ccl:quit)'

The test harness reports failure by SIGNALLING an error ("... tests
failed."). Without --batch, CCL does not exit on an unhandled error in an
--eval: it carries on to the next --eval, and (ccl:quit) exits 0.

Checked directly, on this host:

    ccl --no-init --quiet --eval '(error "x")' --eval '(ccl:quit)'
        -> exit 0          <- the lane as it stands
    ccl --no-init --quiet --batch --eval '(error "x")' --eval '(ccl:quit)'
        -> exit 255

The SBCL lanes do not have this problem: they run with
--non-interactive, under which an unhandled error exits non-zero.

WHAT IT MEANS

NO CCL TEST FAILURE, AND NO CCL LOAD FAILURE, CAN FAIL CI. For either
project. The CCL lanes have been decorative: they run, they print, and
their verdict is ignored.

What it does NOT mean: that the CCL results reported by hand during the
compiler work were wrong. Those were read from the output, by counting
"Fail: [1-9]" lines, not from the exit status -- and clautolisp's CCL
suite is genuinely clean (34 suites, 0 failures, 2026-09-16). alfe's has
exactly one failure, the one in the related ticket.

FIX -- pjb's suggestion, plus a backstop

pjb: "We can call (ccl:quit status) explicitly in ccl." Measured:

    (ccl:quit (handler-case (progn (asdf:test-system ...) 0)
                (error (e) (format *error-output* "~A~%" e) 1)))
        failing run -> exit 1
        passing run -> exit 0

That gives a meaningful code for a TEST failure. It does not, on its own,
cover an error in an EARLIER --eval -- a load-asd or quickload that
fails outside the handler:

    --eval '(error "load failed")' --eval '(ccl:quit (handler-case ...))'
        explicit quit only -> exit 0     <- still silent
        with --batch       -> exit 255

So: the explicit status for the test result, AND --batch so anything
outside the handler still fails the lane. (Wrapping every step in the
one handler-case would also do, but --batch is one word and covers
steps added later that nobody remembers to wrap.)

ORDER MATTERS -- DO NOT LAND THIS ALONE

The moment the alfe CCL lane can fail, it WILL: the failure in
issues/open/alfe-bricscad-template-path-escaped-on-ccl.issue is real and
deterministic. Landing this first turns master red. Fix that one first,
or in the same change.

clautolisp's CCL lane is expected to stay green when this lands -- its
suite had no failures when checked by hand -- which is itself worth
confirming the first time the lane can actually say otherwise.

GITHUB MIRROR

.github/workflows/ci.yml mirrors the Linux lanes by hand. If it invokes
CCL the same way, it has the same blindness; check it when fixing, per
the parity rule.
